-- title:
--   Second node residue equals the reduction of g(V)
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a nonzero level $N$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$; fix modular polynomial data `data` for $q$ satisfying the Kronecker congruence `hKr`, integrality hypotheses $h\alpha$, $h\beta$ for the two degeneracy maps $\overline{F}_N \to \overline{F}_{Nq}$, a place specialisation $P$ of these data, and a prolongation tuple $R$ over $P$. Assume $q \nmid N$, that $R$ satisfies `IsModel` (the two divisor laws and the two cusp laws), the fixed-place order law `OrderLawFixed`, and, for a finite set $W$ of places of `modularFunctionFieldC k N` all lying in `ssPlaces q N k` (rational affine geometric places whose $j$-value is supersingular), the regularity law and the node-value law at $W$. Let $K \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ be a number field, $w \in W$, and suppose the value-integrality law holds at $w$: $V.\mathrm{evalAt}\,f \in A$ for every $f$ in the node ring `R.nodeIntegers w` and every place $V$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar (N * q)` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $P.\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V = w$. Let $g$ lie in `R.nodeIntegersOver K w`, i.e. in `R.nodeIntegers w` with underlying Laurent series in the subfield generated by $K$-constants, $j$ and $j_N$, and let $V$ be a place with $P.\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V = w$. Then the place $\mathrm{arithFrobC}\,q\,k\,N \cdot w$, obtained by acting on $w$ with the coefficientwise $q$-power Frobenius, has the element `R.nodeResidue₂ w g` of `modularFunctionFieldC k N` in its valuation subring, with residue the image of $red(V.\mathrm{evalAt}\,g)$.
--
--   This is the value–residue compatibility at a supersingular node in its second component: reducing the characteristic-zero value $g(V)$ along $red$ computes the value of the second node residue of $g$ at the Frobenius translate of $w$. It feeds the computation of crossing exponents at supersingular points and the construction of pairs of residues with prescribed values on the two branches.
